# Network phase N6: the status page (`httpd`), reached from the host like a
# browser on the local network would. Off unless switched on.
@net
@disk
@forward
$ net dhcp
~ ^address 10\.0\.2\.15/24 \(DHCP\)
$ httpd
status page: off (httpd on [PORT] starts it)
# Off: the port is closed.
@request GET /
(no answer)
$ httpd on
status page on, port 80 (saved)
anyone who can reach this machine can read it, and the files in /public, without a password
$ threads
~ httpd      waiting
@request GET /
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8
Connection: close
~ <title>BeeCodersOs status</title>
~ <tr><th>version</th><td>[\d.]+ b\d+</td></tr>
~ <tr><th>uptime</th><td>0m \d\ds</td></tr>
~ <tr><th>storage</th><td>disk: \d+ of \d+ KiB used</td></tr>
~ <tr><th>main</th><td>
~ <tr><th>httpd</th><td>running
# Recent events: command names and outcomes, never what was typed after them.
~ <td>command threads: ok</td>
~ <td>command httpd: ok</td>
~ <td>command net: ok</td>
!~ 10\.0\.2\.
~ No files are shared\. Files put in /public appear here\.
# Threads: 4 since the desktop clock got a thread of its own (clock.rs).
@request GET /status.json
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
~ ^\{"os":"BeeCodersOs","version":"[\d.]+","build":"\d+","uptime_ms":\d+,"time":"[^"]+","slot":"","on_trial":false,"threads":4,"heap_used_kib":\d+\}$
# Files: only what is in /public, by plain name.
$ mkdir /public
$ echo shared with the network > /public/hello.txt
$ echo <b>not markup</b> > "/public/a b&c.txt"
$ echo private > /secret.txt
$ mkdir /public/sub
$ echo deeper > /public/sub/inner.txt
@request GET /files/hello.txt
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Length: 24
shared with the network
@request GET /
~ <a href="/files/a%20b%26c\.txt">a b&amp;c\.txt</a></th><td>\d+ bytes</td>
~ <a href="/files/hello\.txt">hello\.txt</a></th><td>24 bytes</td>
!~ inner
!~ secret
@request GET /files/a%20b%26c.txt
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
<b>not markup</b>
@request HEAD /files/hello.txt
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Length: 24
!~ shared
# No way out of /public, and nothing below it.
@request GET /files/../secret.txt
HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
!~ private
@request GET /files/..%2Fsecret.txt
HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
!~ private
@request GET /files/sub/inner.txt
HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
@request GET /files/sub
HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
@request GET /files/
HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
@request GET /secret.txt
HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
@request GET /system/log/events.jsonl
HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
# Nothing can be changed through it, and nonsense gets a short answer.
@request POST /
HTTP/1.1 405 Method Not Allowed
@request DELETE /files/hello.txt
HTTP/1.1 405 Method Not Allowed
$ cat /public/hello.txt
shared with the network
@request raw this is not http\r\n\r\n
HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request
@request raw GET /%zz HTTP/1.1\r\n\r\n
HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request
# A client that connects and never finishes its request is dropped after 3 s;
# the next one is served.
@request raw GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: slow
(no answer)
@request GET /status.json
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
$ httpd
~ ^status page: on, http://10\.0\.2\.15/ \(\d+ requests answered\)$
# The setting survives a reboot, in both directions.
@reboot
~ ^\[httpd\] status page on port 80
$ net dhcp
@request GET /status.json
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
$ httpd off
status page off (saved)
@request GET /
(no answer)
$ threads
!~ httpd
@reboot
!~ httpd
$ httpd on 0
~ ^usage: httpd on \[PORT\]
